+# Veterinarian Search Stays Out of Scope, Narrowed to Admit Filtering by a Displayed Attribute
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared that only owners are searchable — searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian was out of scope, on the reasoning that owner search demonstrates paged prefix search once and repeating it elsewhere adds surface, not understanding. The row was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey, so whether it was deliberately declined stayed part of the open provenance consultation.
+
+A request has now asked to filter the veterinarian directory to a single specialty — an attribute the directory already displays. This forces the boundary: is that "searching for a veterinarian", and so barred by NG-9, or is it a different capability the row never meant to exclude?
+
+The owner has decided (2026-08-24).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Read the filter as barred by NG-9.** Rejected: NG-9's reason is that repeating free-text prefix search teaches nothing new. Filtering a listed collection by a value it already shows is not that pattern — it narrows a display, it does not search a corpus.
+2. **Drop NG-9 entirely.** Rejected: free-text veterinarian search — typing a name and matching a corpus — genuinely repeats owner search and still teaches nothing new. The row's core stands.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** (chosen): free-text veterinarian search stays out; filtering the directory by an attribute it already displays is admitted as a distinct, in-scope capability.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed. Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian remains out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory to a single specialty — a value the directory already presents — is in scope, realized by REQ-VET-003. The distinction is corpus search versus attribute filter: the former repeats owner search, the latter narrows an already-listed collection.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open provenance consultation as a decided, narrowed row; every other underived row remains part of it.
+- The veterinarian directory gains an optional specialty filter across both of its surfaces (REQ-VET-003). No free-text veterinarian search is planned.
+- Extending the filter to other attributes, or to other entities, is a fresh owner decision recorded at intake — this ADR admits specialty filtering only.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed row.
+- [REQ-VET-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the specialty filter this narrowing admits.
